{"cuisine/peruvian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/peruvian-cuisine","n":"Peruvian cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Peru","intro":[["Peruvian cuisine refers to the culinary and gastronomical traditions of Peru, which originated in the pre-Columbian indigenous populations in its three main geographical regions along the Pacific coast, the Andes and the Amazonian areas. Some of the fare blends traditions that came after the arrival of the Spaniards in the 1530s, with immigrants from Europe (Spanish cuisine, Italian cuisine, and German cuisine), Asia (Chinese cuisine and Japanese cuisine), the Middle East, and Africa (Maghrebi cuisine and West African cuisine). Lacking ingredients often available in their original countries, these immigrants blended their recipes with those locally available in Peru."],["The four traditional staples of Peruvian cuisine are corn; potatoes and other tubers; Amaranthaceae (quinoa, kañiwa and kiwicha); and legumes (beans and lupins). Staples brought by the Spanish include rice, wheat and meats (beef, pork and chicken)."],["Many traditional foods—such as quinoa, kiwicha, chili peppers, and several roots and tubers—have increased in popularity in recent decades, reflecting a revival of interest in native Peruvian foods and culinary techniques. Chef Gastón Acurio has become well known for raising awareness of local ingredients. The most important ingredient in Peruvian cuisine is the potato, as Peru has the widest variety of potatoes in the world."],["American food critic Eric Asimov has described it as one of the world's most important cuisines and an exemplar of fusion cuisine, due to its long multicultural history."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"peruvian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peruvian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":7,"links":[["Anticucho","dish/anticucho"],["Chicha morada","dish/chicha-morada"],["Coca tea","dish/coca-tea"],["Locro","dish/locro"],["Peruvian ceviche","dish/peruvian-ceviche"],["Picarones","dish/picarones"],["Stuffed peppers","dish/stuffed-peppers"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Peru Delights","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"perudelights.com","note":"Morena Cuadra's blog of Peruvian home recipes and food culture.","url":"https://perudelights.com/tag/peruvian-home-cooking/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Eat Peru","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"eatperu.com","note":"Guide to Peruvian dishes and ingredients with a recipe section.","url":"https://www.eatperu.com/category/recipes/?sf_paged=2","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Peru: The Cookbook","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Gastón Acurio, 2015","note":"Large collection of Peruvian home recipes from Peru's best-known chef.","url":"https://www.phaidon.com/en-us/products/peru-the-cookbook/","suggested":true,"isbn":"9780714869209"},{"name":"The Fire of Peru","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Ricardo Zarate","note":"Peruvian dishes adapted for home kitchens by a Lima-born chef working in the US.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/books/OL27190726M/The_fire_of_Peru","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Capsicum chinense","ingredient/capsicum-chinense"],["Offal","ingredient/offal"],["Purple corn","ingredient/purple-corn"],["Coca leaf","ingredient/coca-leaf"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Beef jerky","ingredient/beef-jerky"],["Cayenne pepper","ingredient/cayenne-pepper"],["Chorizo","ingredient/chorizo"],["Hot sauce","ingredient/hot-sauce"],["Maize","ingredient/maize"],["Paprika","ingredient/paprika"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Zapallo","ingredient/zapallo"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Chancaca","ingredient/chancaca"],["Cucurbita maxima","ingredient/cucurbita-maxima"],["Fig","ingredient/fig"],["Panela","ingredient/panela"],["Squash (plant)","ingredient/squash-plant"],["Sweet potato","ingredient/sweet-potato"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Bell pepper","ingredient/bell-pepper"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Within Peruvian cooking","count":5,"links":[["Amazonian cuisine","cuisine/amazonian-cuisine"],["Arequipan cuisine","cuisine/arequipan-cuisine"],["Inca cuisine","cuisine/inca-cuisine"],["Peruvian-Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chifa"],["Peruvian-Japanese cuisine","cuisine/nikkei-cuisine"]]},{"h":"More from Peru","count":1,"links":[["Quechua foodways","cuisine/quechua-foodways"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/c/c2/Buffet_Costa_Verde.jpg/500px-Buffet_Costa_Verde.jpg","w":500,"h":890,"by":"MiguelAlanCS","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Buffet_Costa_Verde.jpg"}},"cuisine/norwegian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/norwegian-cuisine","n":"Norwegian cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Norway","intro":[["Norwegian cuisine in its traditional form is based largely on the raw materials readily available in Norway. It differs in many respects from continental cuisine with a stronger focus on game and fish. Many of the traditional dishes are the result of using conserved materials because of the long winters."],["Modern Norwegian cuisine, although still strongly influenced by its traditional background, has been influenced by globalization: pasta, spicebags, pizza, burgers, fries, soda, martinis, tacos, and the like are as common as meatballs and cod as staple foods."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"norwegian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norwegian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":8,"links":[["Fish and chips","dish/fish-and-chips"],["Fruktsoppa","dish/fruktsoppa