n","n":"Ayran","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Balkan cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Ayran ( eye-RAHN, also ayryan) is a cold savory yogurt-based beverage found throughout Central Asia, the Balkans, and the Middle East. The drink is a heterogeneous mixture that has been consumed by Turkic peoples for approximately 1,000 years. The principal ingredients are yogurt, water and salt. Herbs such as mint may be added, and some varieties are carbonated. It is a national drink of Turkey and is served in most restaurants in Bulgaria."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-ayran"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ayran"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Balkan cuisine","cuisine/balkan-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Yogurt","ingredient/yogurt"]]},{"h":"More from Balkan cuisine","links":[["Ajvar","dish/ajvar"],["Stuffed tomatoes","dish/stuffed-tomatoes"],["Tarator","dish/tarator"],["Torshi","dish/torshi"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/8/8e/Fresh_ayran.jpg","w":500,"h":674,"by":"Mavigogun","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fresh_ayran.jpg"}},"ingredient/machaca":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/machaca","n":"Machaca","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Machaca is a traditionally dried meat, usually spiced beef or pork, that is rehydrated and then used in popular local cuisine in Northern Mexico and the Southwestern United States.  It is also readily available in many groceries and supermarkets in these areas. In areas where the dried meat product is not easy to obtain, slow-cooked roast beef (brisket) or skirt steak shredded and then fried is sometimes substituted."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-machaca"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Machaca"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Chimichanga","dish/chimichanga"]]],["Mexican cuisine","cuisine/mexican-cuisine",[["Chimichanga","dish/chimichanga"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Adobada","ingredient/adobada"],["Bean","ingredient/bean"],["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Flour tortilla","ingredient/flour-tortilla"],["Jalapeño","ingredient/jalapeno"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Tortilla","ingredient/tortilla"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/45/Machaca_%2832157311937%29.jpg/500px-Machaca_%2832157311937%29.jpg","w":500,"h":667,"by":"Dale Cruse from San Francisco, CA, USA","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Machaca_(32157311937).jpg"}},"dish/doujiang":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/doujiang","n":"Doujiang","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Doujiang is fresh soy milk in Chinese cuisine. It can be served hot or cool, sweet or savoury. Sometimes, it is lightly curdled with vinegar. It is a common breakfast item served with youtiao."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-doujiang"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doujiang"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"],["Taiwan cuisine","cuisine/taiwan-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Soybean","ingredient/soybean"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"The Woks of Life","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"thewoksoflife.com","note":"Chinese and Chinese American recipes from the Leung family, running since 2013.","url":"https://thewoksoflife.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Chinese Cooking Demystified","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"youtube.com","note":"Technique-heavy YouTube channel on regi